{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfhTtgP5DJxqEHkeu1yyP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've written the candidate. It passes the self-check and prints `OK`, but the smoke fit raised two warnings that could still get it rejected at full admission.\n\n**Hypothesis (`negative_recency_expectation_fit`):** people read each sequence flip by flip and compare each flip with a gambler's-fallacy expectation. After a streak, they expect the coin to switch, and they expect it more the longer the streak has run. A sequence looks random to the extent its flips match that expectation, and flips near the end count more. People also differ in how strongly this match drives their choice.\n\nNone of the current models is built this way: they all score a sequence as a whole (balance, motifs, how hard it is to encode, how often it occurs). This one predicts the next flip in order. It targets the critique's discrepancies that survive the FDR correction:\n- **People penalise a sequence ending in a repeat more than the best model predicts:** the extra weight on late flips covers this.\n- **People penalise long streaks more:** the expected chance of a switch rises with streak length.\n- **People favour alternation when balance is nearly tied:** each switch earns credit under the expectation.\n- **People differ from each other more than one shared model allows:** each person gets their own sensitivity, from 400 slots so new participants can be predicted.\n\nThere are five shared parameters plus that per-person sensitivity.\n\n**Speed fix:** my first version used 56 separate data inputs and was still compiling or sampling after about 10 minutes, which risked the 30-minute admission limit. I stopped waiting on it and rewrote the model. The current version passes one index per sequence into a fixed table of all 510 sequences of length 2–8. It finished the self-check in about 6 minutes, most of which was compiling and starting up.\n\n**Self-check:** the smoke fit had a finite ELPD-LOO of about −1055, against −1260 for the current best model. That figure comes from only 100 draws on one chain, so it isn't comparable yet. The two risks for the full fit:\n- **Unreliable PSIS-LOO:** 11.9% of trials had Pareto k > 0.5, well above the 1% tolerance. This is typical when a model has a parameter per person, and with 100 draws the estimate is rough. If it stays unreliable in the full fit, the model can be admitted but cannot be chosen as the exported winner.\n- **Divergences:** there were 2 divergent transitions in 100 draws. The convergence gate allows at most 0.1%, so if this rate holds in the full fit the model would be rejected.\n\nOne more thing: I couldn't stop my first check process because the `kill` command was denied permission. It may still be running inside the sandbox and using CPU.\n\nFiles are in `/scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run3/data/subjective_randomness/experiment1/model_loop/iter_0/candidate_0/`:\n- `hypothesis.md`\n- `model_name.txt`\n- `candidate.py`"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1160,"cache_read_input_tokens":60538,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1160,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"73813b53-8bc9-451b-a34c-64df5c8dba30","uuid":"492afc52-0864-4406-b016-8565d2d1d883","timestamp":"2026-10-04T15:52:03.998Z","request_id":"req_011CfhTtfzFg6RBUoVDxefjY"}
